<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="sec_1_4">

    
<title>Vertical and Horizontal Intercepts</title>


<objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            After completing this section, you should be able to do the following.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <ol>
        <li>
            <p>
            First objective.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
        Second objective.
            </p>
        </li> 
        <li>
            <p>
            Third objective.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>
</objectives>


<p>
The points where the graph of a function crosses the horizontal axis or the vertical axis are of special interest.
They are called the horizontal and the vertical intercepts.
If the independent variable is named <m>x</m> and the dependent variable is named <m>y</m>, the horizontal and vertical intercepts are also called <m>x</m>-intercepts and <m>y</m>-intercepts, respectively.
</p>



  <image width="50%" xml:id="fig_1_4_1">
    <shortdescription>
    A short description.
    </shortdescription>
    <latex-image label="1_4_1_fig">
    \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
    \begin{axis}[grid=both,
    xmin=-1,xmax=3,
    label style={font=\large},
    ymin=-1,ymax=3,
    xtick=\empty,
    ytick=\empty,
    minor tick={},
    xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
    ]
    \node[rpoint with label={90:{\large $(x,0)$}}] at (axis cs:2,0) {};
    \node[rpoint with label={0:{\large $(0,y)$}}] at (axis cs:0,2) {};
    \end{axis}
    \end{tikzpicture}
    </latex-image>
</image>

<p>
The plot above illustrates that every point in the <m>xy</m>-plane that lies on the <m>y</m>-axis has the <m>x</m>-coordinate <m>0</m>, and every point that lies on <m>x</m>-axis has the <m>y</m>-coordinate <m>0</m>.
We will use this observation to find the vertical and horizontal intercepts (if they exist).
</p>

<definition><title>Vertical Intercept</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
        Suppose the graph of the function <m>y=f(x)</m> crosses the <m>y</m>-axis at <m>y=b</m>; that is, at the point <m>(0,b)</m>.
        Then <m>b</m> is called the <m>y</m>-intercept of the function <m>f</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>


<p>
Since <m>(0,b)</m> is on the graph of the function, <m>b=f(0)</m>.
So the <m>y</m>-intercept is simply the value of the function at <m>x=0</m>.
</p>

<aside>
<p>
Why can a function not have more than one vertical intercept? Hint: Consider the vertical line test.
</p>
</aside>

<p>
If <m>x=0</m> is in the domain of <m>f</m>, we have exactly one <m>y</m>-intercept.
If <m>x=0</m> is not in the domain, the graph of <m>f</m> does not cross the <m>y</m>-axis and there is no <m>y</m>-intercept.
</p>

<p>
    That is: <em>To find the vertical intercept of <m>f(x)</m>, evaluate <m>f(0)</m></em>.
</p>


<image width="50%" xml:id="fig_1_4_2">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="1_4_2_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\begin{axis}[grid=none,
xmin=-1,xmax=3.5,
ymin=-1,ymax=3,
xtick=\empty,
ytick=\empty,
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\)
]
\addplot[domain=-1:3] {x^3-3*x^2+2*x+1} node[left,pos=0.85] {$y=f(x)$};
\node[rpoint with label={0:{$(0,b)$}}] at (axis cs:0,1) {};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>


<definition><title>Horizontal Intercepts</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
        Suppose that the graph of the function <m>y=f(x)</m> crosses the <m>x</m>-axis at <m>x=a</m>; that is, at the point <m>(a,0)</m>.
        Then <m>a</m> is called an <m>x</m>-intercept of the function <m>f</m>.
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>

<p>
Since the point <m>(a,0)</m> is on the graph of the function, <m>f(a)=0</m>.
So the <m>x</m>-intercepts are the real values of <m>x</m> at which <m>f(x)=0</m>.
A function may have many <m>x</m>-intercepts.
They are often called the <em>zeros</em> or <em>roots</em> of a graphed function <m>f</m>.
</p>

<p>
That is: <em>To find horizontal intercepts of <m>f(x)</m>, solve <m>f(x)=0</m> for <m>x</m>.</em>
</p>


<image width="50%" xml:id="fig_1_4_3">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="1_4_3_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\begin{axis}[grid=none,
xmin=-1,xmax=3.5,
ymin=-1,ymax=3,
xtick=\empty,
ytick=\empty,
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\)
]
\addplot[domain=-1:3] {-0.1*x^3 - 0.1*x^2 + 1} node[left,pos=0.85] {$y=f(x)$};
\node[rpoint with label={45:{$(a,0)$}}] at (axis cs:1.86746,0) {};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>


<example>
<statement>
    <p>
    Consider the function <m>y=f(x)</m> where <m>f(x)=x^{2}-4</m>.
    Find the vertical intercept and the horizontal intercepts.
    </p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
To find the <m>y</m>-intercept, we evaluate <m>f(0)=0^{2}-4=-4</m>.
Therefore, <m>f(x)</m> crosses the <m>y</m>-axis at <m>y=-4</m>; that is, the vertical intercept is the point <m>(0,-4)</m>.
</p>

<aside>
<p>
In describing the vertical intercept of a function, we might say it is at <m>y=-4</m> or at <m>(0,-4)</m>.
Keep in mind that these represent the same concept.
</p>
</aside>

<p>
To find <m>x</m>-intercepts, we have to solve the equation <m>f(x)=0</m>.
For our function, the equation we must solve is <m>x^{2}-4=0</m>.
We use the standard techniques for solving equations.
<md>
<mrow>x^{2}-4 \amp \;=\; 0</mrow>
<mrow>x^{2} \amp \;=\; 4</mrow>
<mrow>x \amp \;=\; \pm\sqrt{4}</mrow>
<mrow>x \amp \;=\; \pm \, 2</mrow>
</md>
There are two solutions, <m>x=-2</m> and <m>x=2</m>.
These are the two horizontal intercepts or <em>zeros</em> of the function.
Since <m>f(-2)=0</m> and <m>f(2)=0</m>, the associated ordered pairs are <m>(-2,0)</m> and <m>(2,0)</m>.
</p>
<aside>
    <p>
    In describing a horizontal intercept of a function, we might say it is at <m>x=2</m> or at <m>(2,0)</m>.
    Keep in mind that these represent the same concept.
    </p>
</aside>

<p>
The graph of the function clearly shows the intercepts and the corresponding points on the <m>xy</m>-plane:
</p>


<image width="50%" xml:id="fig_1_4_4">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="1_4_4_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
xmin=-4.5,xmax=4.5,
ymin=-6,ymax=7,
xtick={-4,...,4},
ytick={-4,-2,2,4,6},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
]
\addplot[domain=-4:4] {x^2-4} node[left,pos=1] {\large $y=x^2-4$};
\node[rpoint with label={135:{$(-2,0)$}}] at (axis cs:-2,0) {};
\node[rpoint with label={45:{$(2,0)$}}] at (axis cs:2,0) {};
\node[rpoint with label={-45:{$(0,-4)$}}] at (axis cs:0,-4) {};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
</solution>
</example>



<example xml:id="example-ch1-leak">

<statement>
    <p>
      A full tank of water springs a leak.
    Water is leaking out at the rate of <m>3</m> gallons per hour.
    Let <m>A=w(t)</m> be the amount of water in the tank, in gallons, <m>t</m> hours after the leak started.The function <m>w(t)</m> is given by
    <md>
    A=w(t)=60-3t.
    </md>
    Find vertical and horizontal intercepts of the function <m>A=w(t)</m> and interpret them in practical terms.
    Also graph the function.  
    </p>
</statement>

<solution>
    <p>
For the vertical intercept<mdash/>or the <m>A</m>-intercept<mdash/>of the function <m>A=w(t)</m>, we evaluate <m>w(0)</m>, which results in <m>A=60</m>.
Since <m>60</m> is the value of <m>A</m> at <m>t=0</m>, it is measured in gallons and it gives the initial amount of water in the tank.
We conclude that the vertical intercept of 60 gallons is the initial amount of water in the tank.
Note that 60 gallons is also the capacity of the tank as the tank was full when the leak began at time <m>t=0</m>.
    </p>

<p>
To find horizontal intercepts, we have to solve the equation <m>w(t)=0</m> for <m>t</m>; that is,
<md>
<mrow>60-3t \amp \;=\; 0</mrow>
<mrow>60 \amp \;=\; 3t</mrow>
<mrow>20 \amp \;=\; t</mrow>
<mrow>t \amp \;=\; 20</mrow>
</md>
The function has one horizontal intercept at <m>t=20</m>.
Thus, <m>20</m> hours after the leak started, the amount of water <m>A(20)</m> left in the tank is <m>A=w(20)=0</m>.
In practical terms, it will take <m>20</m> hours until the tank is empty.
</p>

<p>
Here is the graph of the function <m>A=w(t)</m>.
The intercepts are clearly visible on the graph and their meaning is clear as well.
</p>

<image width="50%" xml:id="fig_1_4_5">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="1_4_5_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
xmin=0,xmax=4.5,
ymin=0,ymax=6.5,
xtick={1,2,3,4},
xticklabels={5,10,15,20},
ytick={1,2,3,4,5,6},
yticklabels={10,20,30,40,50,60},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(t\), ylabel=\(A\),
]
\addplot[domain=0:4] {-1.5*x+6};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
</solution>
</example>



<p>
Given the graph of a function, with its formula unknown, we are still able to find the intercepts.
</p>


<example>
<statement>
    <p>
    Use the graph of a function <m>y=f(x)</m> below to estimate its vertical and horizontal intercepts.
    </p>

<image width="50%" xml:id="fig_1_4_6">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="1_4_6_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
xmin=-3,xmax=5,
ymin=-11,ymax=11,
xtick={-2,-1,1,2,3,4},
ytick={-10,-8,-6,-4,-2,2,4,6,8},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
x post scale = 1.2,
]
\addplot[domain=-3:5] {-1*(x+2)*(x-1)*(x-4)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
</statement>

<solution>
    <p>
The vertical intercept is where the graph of a function crosses the vertical axis, which is the <m>y</m>-axis for the function shown.
We can see that this is the ordered pair <m>(0,-8)</m>.
Thus, the vertical intercept occurs at <m>y=-8</m>.
    </p>

<p>
The graph crosses the <m>x</m>-axis at each of <m>x=-2,1,4</m>.
These are the <m>x</m>-intercepts (the zeros) of <m>f(x)</m>, meaning <m>f(x)=0</m> for each of <m>x=-2,1,4</m>.
The corresponding points are <m>(-2,0)</m>, <m>(1,0)</m>, and <m>(4,0)</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</example>


<p>
Similarly, given a function <m>y=f(x)</m> that is described numerically (i.e., by a table of values), we can find the intercepts by looking for when <m>y=0</m> or <m>x=0</m>.
</p>


<example>
<statement>
    <p>
    Every summer the depth of water in a reservoir is measured weekly over the 10-week period beginning with July 1.
    Let <m>D</m> be the depth of water, in meters, and <m>t</m> denote the time, in weeks, since July 1.
    Here, <m>D</m> is a function of <m>t</m>, which can be written as <m>D=D(t)</m>.
    The readings from 2019 are provided in the table below.
</p>

<table xml:id="table-ch1-depth-water">
    <title></title>
 <tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
    <row>
    <cell> <m>t</m> (weeks)</cell>
    <cell> <m>0</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>1</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>2</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>3</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>4</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>5</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>6</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>7</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>8</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>9</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>10</m> </cell>
    </row>
    <row>
    <cell> <m>D(t)</m> (meters)</cell>
    <cell> <m>10</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>8</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>7</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>4</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>2</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>0</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>3</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>4</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>3</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>0</m> </cell>
    <cell> <m>4</m> </cell>
    </row>
    </tabular>
</table>

<p>
What are the vertical and horizontal intercepts given by the table? What is their practical meaning? Verify your answer on a graph of <m>D(t)</m>.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
    <p>
    The vertical intercept occurs when <m>t=0</m> and hence has value given by <m>D(0)</m>.
    Per the table, <m>D(0)=10</m>.
    Hence, the vertical intercept, or <m>D</m>-intercept, is <m>10</m>.
    In practical terms, the vertical intercept tells us that the water depth on July 1, 2019 was 10 meters.
</p>

<p>
The horizontal intercepts are values of <m>t</m> for which <m>D(t)=0</m>.
    The depth is <m>0</m> at <m>t=5</m> and <m>t=9</m>.
    In practical terms, the horizontal intercepts tell us that at both 5 and 9 weeks after July 1, 2019, the reservoir was empty.
    Below is a graph that fits the data from <xref ref="table-ch1-depth-water"/>.
    We can clearly see the intercepts.
</p>

<image width="50%" xml:id="fig_1_4_7">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="1_4_7_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
xmin=0,xmax=11,
ymin=0,ymax=11,
xtick={1,...,10},
ytick={1,...,10},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(t\), ylabel=\(D\),
x post scale = 1
]
\addplot+[smooth,mark=*,mark options={fill=red}] plot coordinates {
(0,10)
(1,8)
(2,7) 
(3,4)
(4,2)
(5,0)
(6,3)
(7,4)
(8,3)
(9,0)
(10,4)
};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>
</solution>
</example>




